  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1256 AM MDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nIt is said that the weather pattern often repeats itself. And the \npattern for this week is no exception. The past couple of heat waves \nwe had, the warmest temperatures occur over the weekend. Hot \ntemperatures linger into early the next week. And then, monsoonal \nmoisture really begins to increase and with the increased cloud \ncover and precipitation coverage, temperatures drop off, probably to \nbelow normal levels by the end of the week. And, with what happened \nwith the best couple of monsoonal events, the models will handle the \nplacement and timing of the showers rather poorly.\n\nAnd example is tonight, they did have the virga showers in southern \nWyoming. But they had nothing in northern Wyoming. The also did not \nhave anything lingering across the south after midnight, and we have \nthat as well. However, as for today, the large majority of the area \nwill have another hot and dry day, although somewhat more cloud \ncover could lower temperatures a degree or two. It will still be \nabove normal across most of the area though. And like yesterday, \ncritical fire weather is likely across most of the area with \nhumidity remaining below 15 percent and a gusty breeze developing in \nthe afternoon. As for convection, we did increase coverage somewhat, \nexpanding it into the southern Wind River Basin this afternoon. Like \nyesterday, with the very dry low levels, it would likely only be \nvirga. However, with the large dew point depressions (up to 70 \ndegrees in some locations), we could have some strong wind gusts, \npossibly over 50 mph. This has the potential to be a \"Little Green \nBlob\" day. Coverage still looks sparse though, generally less than a \n1 out of 4 chance. And, we will continue to have areas of smoke \nbringing poor air quality.\n\nMonday and Tuesday look fairly similar. With 700 millibar \ntemperatures still in the 18 to 19 celsius range, it will continue to \nbe rather hot, with temperatures similar to today's highs. As for \nconvection, there is actually decent model agreement in keeping the \nbulk of the monsoonal moisture south of Wyoming. The main area of \nconcern would be across southern Wyoming, but even here chances look \nfairly small, generally less than a 1 out of 4 chance. I can't rule \none out in some of the normal development areas, like the Washakie \nNeedles at the southern end of the Absarokas, but with the chance \nless than 1 out of 6 we kept the forecast dry for now. As for fire \nweather concerns, there could still be some. Relative humidity will \nstill be in the teens but a bit higher than over the weekend. Wind \nalso looks borderline. So, we will not extend the warnings at this \ntime, but future shifts may want to take another look at things.\n\nThe pattern begins to shift on Wednesday. A weak cold front will \napproach from the northeast and bring cooler temperatures starting \non Wednesday but especially for the end of the week, with below \nnormal temperatures for many from Thursday into next week. And, \nmoisture will increase starting on Wednesday. This will bring an \nincreased chance of showers and thunderstorms across the area into \nthe weekend. Precipitable waters look to rise above 150 percent of \nnormal starting especially on Thursday and continue into the \nweekend. At this point, it does not look as moist as during the \nmiddle of July, with precipitable waters maxing out at around 25 \npercent less than that time period. As for details, they are still \nuncertain. The monsoon will be enhanced by a few shortwaves passing \nthrough but these are difficult to time, especially toward the end \nof the week. There is some agreement with most activity bring East \nof the Divide on Wednesday, past that time details become far more \nuncertain in regards to placement and day of most numerous activity. \nAs for possibly heavy rain and flooding, there is one difference. \nMid level flow will be somewhat stronger than a few weeks ago, with \n20 to 35 knots of flow at 500 millibars. This should allow storms to \nhave more movement and reduce the risk somewhat. This, along with \nfact that the recent dry weather means there is plenty of room in \nthe soil. However, we do need the rain and we will take what we can \nget, as long it is not too much all at once.",
